<title>6.1 Practice Worksheet</title>


<introduction>
    <p>
    <url href="https://youtu.be/fmrt29pDOMs">Click here for a walkthrough video of some problem below.</url>
    </p>
</introduction>



<exercisegroup>
<title>Evaluating Logarithms</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Evaluate without using a calculator, or say that the expression is undefined.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\ln (e^9)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
If <m>\ln (e^9)=x</m> then <m>e^x = e^9</m>, so <m>x</m> must be <m>9</m>. Hence <m>\ln (e^9)=9</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log_4 (\sqrt{4^3})</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
If <m>\log_4(\sqrt{4^3} )=x</m> then <m>4^x = \sqrt{4^3} </m>. Note that <m>\sqrt{4^3} = (4^3)^{1 /2} = 4^{3 /2}</m>. Therefore <m>4^x = 4^{3 /2}</m> and thus <m>x=\frac{3}{2}</m> is the value of the logarithm.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log (0.0001)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<em>Recall: <m>\log </m> with no subscript means base <m>10</m></em>
</p>

<p>
Because <m>0.0001 = 10^{-4}</m>, then this is <m>\log (10^{-4})</m>. If <m>\log (10^{-4})=x</m>, then <m>10^x = 10^{-4}</m>. Therefore <m>x=-4</m> and so <m>\log (0.0001)=-4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log_2(-8)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This is not defined. Recall that <m>\log _b(x)</m> is only defined for <m>x \gt 0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log_7 \pbr{\dfrac{1}{343}}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that <m>343 = 7^3</m> so that <m>\frac{1}{343}=7^{-3}</m>. Therefore <m>\log_7(7^{-3})=x</m> means that <m>7^x = 7^{-3}</m> and so <m>x=-3</m>, implying that the logarithm value is <m>-3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log_4(16)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because <m>16=4^2</m>, then we have <m>\log _4(4^2)</m>. If <m>\log _4(4^2)=x</m>, then <m>4^x=4^2</m> and so <m>x=2</m>. Therefore <m>\log _4(16)=2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\ln \pbr{\dfrac{e^2}{\sqrt[3]{e}}}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We can simplify the fraction here to be
<md>
\frac{e^2}{\sqrt[3]{e} } \quad = \quad \frac{e^2}{e^{1 /3}}  \quad = \quad e^{2-1/3} \quad = \quad e^{5/3} .
</md> Now, we are computing <m>\ln (e^{5 /3})</m>. If <m>\ln (e^{5 /3})=x</m>, then <m>e^x = e^{5 /3}</m> so <m>x=\frac{5}{3}</m> is the logarithm value.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\log_9 \pbr{\dfrac{1}{6561}}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that <m>6561 = 9^4</m> and so <m>\frac{1}{6561} = 9^{-4}</m>. Therefore we have <m>\log _9(9^{-4})</m> and this is just <m>-4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Use the definition of the logarithm to solve each equation.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>2^x=63</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With the definition of the logarithm, then <m>\log _2(63)=x</m> is the exact solution. As a decimal, this is <m>x=5.97728</m> (do this on your calculator).
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>6(10)^x-4=5</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Adding <m>4</m> to both sides, then <m>6 \cdot 10^x = 9</m>. Dividing by <m>6</m> yields <m>10^x = \frac{9}{6}=\frac{3}{2}</m>. Now, by the definition of the logarithm this means that <m>\log(3 /2) = x</m>. Recall that <m>\log </m> with no subscript is base <m>10</m>. As a decimal, <m>x=0.17609</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>


</worksheet>